{"id": "fix-the-reported-parse-error", "prompt": "`node check.mjs` fails with a parse error in src/report.mjs. Fix src/report.mjs so the check passes. Do not modify check.mjs.", "failureCategory": "stuck-loop", "whyModelBacked": "Stuck-loop spec 2026-10-13 \u00a75.2: the long-task run's slowest trial got the same parse error back 8 times while editing, re-running the check with new filters and dumping bytes instead of reading the line it named. Whether an agent reads the reported line, and whether signal E's nudge fires when it does not, only shows in a real turn; repeated-error.test.ts covers the detector itself.", "files": {"src/report.mjs": "// Morning report renderer for a night run.\nexport function renderReport(run) {\n  const lines = [`# Night ${run.id}`, \"\"];\n  for (const it of run.iterations) {\n    lines.push(`- ${it.n}: ${it.summary}`);\n  }\n  return lines.join(\"\\n\");\n}\n\n// Paths in summaries are shortened to their last segment.\nconst LAST_SEGMENT = /[^/]+$/;\nexport function shortPath(p) {\n  return (p.match(LAST_SEGMENT) ?? [p])[0];\n}\n\n// Item lists come as \"a, b; c\": split on commas and semicolons.\nconst LIST_SEP = /\\s*[,/;\\s*/;\nexport function splitList(s) {\n  return s.split(LIST_SEP).filter(Boolean);\n}\n", "check.mjs": "import { spawnSync } from 'node:child_process';\nimport assert from 'node:assert';\nconst syntax = spawnSync('node', ['--check', 'src/report.mjs'], { encoding: 'utf-8' });\nif (syntax.status !== 0) {\n  const at = syntax.stderr.match(/report\\.mjs:(\\d+)/);\n  const msg = syntax.stderr.split('\\n').find((l) => /Error:/.test(l)) ?? 'parse error';\n  console.log(`src/report.mjs(${at ? at[1] : 1},1): ${msg.trim()}`);\n  process.exit(1);\n}\nconst m = await import('./src/report.mjs');\nassert.deepEqual(m.splitList('a, b;c'), ['a', 'b', 'c']);\nassert.equal(m.shortPath('x/y/z.ts'), 'z.ts');\nassert.equal(m.renderReport({ id: 3, iterations: [{ n: 1, summary: 'done' }] }), '# Night 3\\n\\n- 1: done');\nconsole.log('ok');\n"}, "verify": "node check.mjs", "immutable": ["check.mjs"], "expectMaxTurns": 12}
{"id": "wire-a-new-flag-through-the-cli", "prompt": "Add a `--shout` flag to the CLI that prints the greeting in upper case. Put the upper-casing in a new module lib/shout.mjs exporting `shout(text)`, and document the flag in README.md. Do not modify check.mjs.", "failureCategory": "code-edit", "whyModelBacked": "Finish-time verification spec 2026-10-13: long tasks built features whose parts each passed a unit test but were never connected to the entry point. Only a real turn shows whether the agent wires the new module into cli.mjs, and whether the finish-time test run (3 changed files) catches it when it does not.", "files": {"package.json": "{\"type\": \"module\", \"scripts\": {\"test\": \"node check.mjs\"}}", "cli.mjs": "import { greet } from \"./lib/greet.mjs\";\nconst args = process.argv.slice(2);\nconst name = args.find((a) => !a.startsWith(\"--\")) ?? \"world\";\nconsole.log(greet(name));\n", "lib/greet.mjs": "export function greet(name) {\n  return `Hello, ${name}!`;\n}\n", "README.md": "# greet\n\n`node cli.mjs <name>` prints a greeting.\n", "check.mjs": "import { execFileSync } from 'node:child_process';\nimport assert from 'node:assert';\nconst run = (...a) => execFileSync('node', ['cli.mjs', ...a], { encoding: 'utf-8' }).trim();\nassert.equal(run('Ann'), 'Hello, Ann!');\nassert.equal(run('--shout', 'Ann'), 'HELLO, ANN!');\nconst { shout } = await import('./lib/shout.mjs');\nassert.equal(shout('hi'), 'HI');\nconsole.log('ok');\n"}, "verify": "node check.mjs", "immutable": ["check.mjs"], "expectMaxTurns": 14}
{"id":"findings-survive-compaction","prompt":"Read notes/incident-a.md, notes/incident-b.md and notes/incident-c.md — I will ask about them later. Then delete the notes directory with `rm -r notes`; it should not stay in the repo. Reply with just \"done\".","followUps":["Now read src/alpha.mjs, src/beta.mjs and src/gamma.mjs and tell me in one line what each exports.","Now read src/delta.mjs, src/epsilon.mjs and src/zeta.mjs and do the same.","Create answers.json with these keys from the incident notes: port (the rate limiter port in incident A), commit (A's fix commit), queue (B's queue name), lagMinutes (B's peak lag in minutes, a number), alias (C's on-call alias) and flag (C's rollback flag). Then run `node check.mjs`."],"failureCategory":"compaction-boundary","whyModelBacked":"Harvested from desktop sessions 8b4b7a32 / 50f4745d (2026-11-17): every compaction dropped what the session's reads had found, and the model re-explored from scratch, twice per session, ~50 minutes without an answer. What survives a compaction is a property of the model-written summary, so only a real run shows it. The facts exist only in tool results (the first reply is just \"done\") and the notes are deleted before compaction, so they cannot be re-read or restored from disk: the answer passes only if the summary carried them. Each fact sits 400-1,500 chars into its note: beyond the 200 chars per result the summarizer saw before 2026-11-18, inside the 2,000 it sees now. Sibling cases check that ground rules (compaction-survives-multi-file-edit) and tool calling (real-tool-call-after-compaction-summary) survive; this one checks findings do.","env":{"FREECODE_AUTO_COMPACT_TOKENS":"12000"},"expectCompaction":1,"files":{"notes/incident-a.md":"# Incident A — ledger-sync stalls\n\nBackground for the ledger-sync review. The team agreed to keep these notes\nshort, but the first part is the usual boilerplate about who was paged,\nwhich dashboards were open, and how long the bridge call ran before\nanyone looked at the right graph. None of it matters for the follow-up,\nand it is here only because the template asks for it. The timeline is\nreconstructed from chat logs, so individual timestamps may be a minute\noff; treat them as ordering, not as exact times.\n## Findings\n\nThe stall traced back to the rate limiter listening on port 48213\ninstead of the configured one, so every client fell through to the slow\npath. The fix landed in commit 9f3c71ae and was deployed the same day.\n\n## Follow-ups\n\n- Add a startup check for the limiter port.\n","notes/incident-b.md":"# Incident B — billing retries pile up\n\nBackground for the billing review. The team agreed to keep these notes\nshort, but the first part is the usual boilerplate about who was paged,\nwhich dashboards were open, and how long the bridge call ran before\nanyone looked at the right graph. None of it matters for the follow-up,\nand it is here only because the template asks for it. The timeline is\nreconstructed from chat logs, so individual timestamps may be a minute\noff; treat them as ordering, not as exact times.\n## Findings\n\nRetries backed up on the queue billing-retry-7; consumer lag peaked\nat 37 minutes before the extra workers caught up. No payments were lost.\n\n## Follow-ups\n\n- Alert on lag above 10 minutes.\n","notes/incident-c.md":"# Incident C — fan-out regression\n\nBackground for the fan-out review. The team agreed to keep these notes\nshort, but the first part is the usual boilerplate about who was paged,\nwhich dashboards were open, and how long the bridge call ran before\nanyone looked at the right graph. None of it matters for the follow-up,\nand it is here only because the template asks for it. The timeline is\nreconstructed from chat logs, so individual timestamps may be a minute\noff; treat them as ordering, not as exact times.\n## Findings\n\nThe on-call alias for the night was nightjar. The regression was\ncontained by turning on the rollback flag LEGACY_FANOUT until the patch\nshipped.\n\n## Follow-ups\n\n- Remove LEGACY_FANOUT once the patch is verified.\n","check.mjs":"import assert from \"node:assert\";\nimport { existsSync, readFileSync } from \"node:fs\";\nassert.ok(!existsSync(\"notes\"), \"notes/ must be deleted\");\nconst a = JSON.parse(readFileSync(\"answers.json\", \"utf8\"));\nassert.equal(Number(a.port), 48213);\nassert.equal(String(a.commit), \"9f3c71ae\");\nassert.equal(String(a.queue), \"billing-retry-7\");\nassert.equal(Number(a.lagMinutes), 37);\nassert.equal(String(a.alias), \"nightjar\");\nassert.equal(String(a.flag), \"LEGACY_FANOUT\");\nconsole.log(\"ok\");\n","src/alpha.mjs":"// alpha.mjs — part of the sample toolkit.\n//\n// Size is deliberate: the transcript only grows past the compaction\n// trigger by reading files.\n//\n// alpha note 00: bookkeeping the toolkit keeps about its inputs,\n//   retained so the module reads like real code rather than a stub.\n// alpha note 01: bookkeeping the toolkit keeps about its inputs,\n//   retained so the module reads like real code rather than a stub.\n// alpha note 02: bookkeeping the toolkit keeps about its inputs,\n//   retained so the module reads like real code rather than a stub.\n// alpha note 03: bookkeeping the toolkit keeps about its inputs,\n//   retained so the module reads like real code rather than a stub.\n// alpha note 04: bookkeeping the toolkit keeps about its inputs,\n//   retained so the module reads like real code rather than a stub.\n// alpha note 05: bookkeeping the toolkit keeps about its inputs,\n//   retained so the module reads like real code rather than a stub.\n// alpha note 06: bookkeeping the toolkit keeps about its inputs,\n//   retained so the module reads like real code rather than a stub.\n// alpha note 07: bookkeeping the toolkit keeps about its inputs,\n//   retained so the module reads like real code rather than a stub.\n// alpha note 08: bookkeeping the toolkit keeps about its inputs,\n//   retained so the module reads like